{
 "text": " In 1932, Australia declared war on birds and lost. 20,000 emus were eating the wheat farms of Western Australia. So the army sent three soldiers, two machine guns, and 10,000 bullets. First, they set an ambush at a watering hole. A huge flock walked right in. Then the gun jammed. Next, they bolted a machine gun onto a truck. The emus just ran. On the rough ground, the truck couldn't keep up and the gunner couldn't aim. The bird split into small groups, each with a lookout. The major in charge said they faced machine guns like tanks. After one week, the army retreated. They came back and claimed 986 kills. That's ten bullets for every bird. It barely made a dent. So farmers went back to bounties and claimed 57,000 in six months. The emus had won the war. Subscribe for more Weird History.",
